  <div class="mt-20 grid gap-12 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:gap-16">
    <section>
      <h2 class="page-display mb-4 mt-0 text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Deeper means calmer.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">Equalise early, turn at the depth you planned, and come up with air to spare.</p>
      <p class="page-prose mb-0">I certify Molchanovs Wave 1 and Wave 2. Always train with a qualified buddy watching and stay well within your limits.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="page-display mb-4 mt-0 text-3xl sm:text-4xl">One-to-one for kids.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose mb-0">An instructor stays beside your child the whole time, and each skill is solid before the next one starts. <a href="/junior/" class="font-bold decoration-sky-200 decoration-8">Freediving for kids</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class="essay-wrap">
  <article class="essay">
    <section id="twelve">
      <h2 class="page-display">First contact.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">I first tried freediving in December 2023. Once I understood that you need to unplug in your head, I held my breath for over four minutes.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Back then I turned around at twelve metres and wondered how anyone stays relaxed past a hundred.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="breath">
      <h2 class="page-display">The urge to breathe.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">The urge comes within a minute. It is carbon dioxide building up. You still have plenty of oxygen.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Let the alarming pictures in your head go by. It feels strange because you expect imminent doom and nothing is happening. Then it turns into meditation, a way to calm down the body and mind together.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Move smoothly and the urge comes later. Trust the water and it stops pushing back.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="letting-go">
      <h2 class="page-display">Letting go.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">You cannot dive angry, hurt or irritated. Freediving is letting go of everything. Every dive should feel easy, and so should life.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">I left every grudge I carried on the seabed off Racha Yai. Free as in freedom.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="wall">
      <h2 class="page-display">The wall.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">The instructor test is a hundred metres underwater on one breath. Seventy-five you can force. The last twenty-five open up only when you let go of the fear.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">On the edge of the pool I was bracing for certain death. My friend Misha said: &ldquo;Seventy-five to a hundred is the hardest stretch. After that it gets easier.&rdquo; I pushed off thinking: this is the worst part, and nothing is going to happen. I came up at a hundred, and the wall was gone.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Certain death was only sensations in the body. The wall is a process. You learn to pass through fear.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="thoughts">
      <h2 class="page-display">Thoughts that are not yours.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">Out in the blue, with tens of metres of water above you, your body and mind get scared and broadcast that fear. You hear it as thoughts in your head, in a voice that sounds exactly like yours.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Settle your nervous system. Nothing is happening. All is well.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="water">
      <h2 class="page-display">Only the water.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">In May 2026 I passed my last instructor test in Bali, and the pressure was off. Forty metres became light. I stopped counting and controlling.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">We live wrapped in a thin film of fear. In Bali it burst. I fell head first and nothing happened. No fear, no discomfort, no me. Only the water, gently holding me.</p>
    </section>
    <section id="land">
      <h2 class="page-display">On land.</h2>
      <p class="page-prose">Now I mostly dive with students, and every dive is a lesson. Depth no longer worries me, so I scan my body for whatever is still tense.</p>
      <p class="page-prose">Life works the same way. It was all in my head. It was only discomfort.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
